--TEST--
`instanceof`'s right-hand side must name a class some descriptor answers to — the dynamic form, an enum and a `Core` namespace class are `E0496`, an undeclared name the ordinary `E0303` — `rule:types/conversion`, `rule:enums/closed-integer-type`
--FILE--
<?nvs
enum Mode: int {
    Read = 1,
    Write = 2,
}

final class Box {
    public int $n = 1;
}

mixed $m = new Box();
string $name = "Box";

// `rule:types/conversion`: a class name is written, never computed — the same line
// `$$var` and `eval` are already on. `rule:types/class-reference-sites` opens exactly one door,
// and a bare `string` is not it: the help names the conversion that is.
bool $a = $m instanceof $name;
// A string literal is the same dynamic form spelled without a variable.
bool $b = $m instanceof "Box";
// `rule:enums/closed-integer-type` makes an enum case a named integer rather than an object, so no
// value is ever an instance of one.
bool $c = $m instanceof Mode;
// `Core\Str` is a namespace for static members: it declares no property and no
// instance member, so no value is ever one and there is no descriptor to walk.
bool $d = $m instanceof Core\Str;
// A name that resolves to nothing at all is the ordinary undeclared-class
// diagnostic, exactly as `new Undeclared()` reports it — one mistake, one
// code, wherever the name is written.
bool $e = $m instanceof Undeclared;
echo "unreached\n";
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0496]: the right-hand side of `instanceof` must be a written class name
   --> %s:17:25
   |
17 | bool $a = $m instanceof $name;
   |                         ^^^^^ not a class name
   = help: Novis has no dynamic class names (`rule:types/conversion`, the rule that rejects `$$var` and `eval`) — write the class, or convert the name once and carry the result: `$name as class<Base>` yields a class reference this site accepts, checked against `Base`'s hierarchy where the conversion stands (`rule:types/class-reference-sites`)

error[E0496]: the right-hand side of `instanceof` must be a written class name
   --> %s:19:25
   |
19 | bool $b = $m instanceof "Box";
   |                         ^^^^^ not a class name
   = help: Novis has no dynamic class names (`rule:types/conversion`, the rule that rejects `$$var` and `eval`) — write the class, or convert the name once and carry the result: `$name as class<Base>` yields a class reference this site accepts, checked against `Base`'s hierarchy where the conversion stands (`rule:types/class-reference-sites`)

error[E0496]: `Mode` is an enum, and no value is ever an instance of one
   --> %s:22:25
   |
22 | bool $c = $m instanceof Mode;
   |                         ^^^^ an enum is a value type
   = help: `rule:enums/closed-integer-type` makes an enum case a named integer rather than an object — compare it with `==`, or `match` on it

error[E0496]: `Core\Str` is a namespace for static members, and has no instances
   --> %s:25:25
   |
25 | bool $d = $m instanceof Core\Str;
   |                         ^^^^^^^^ no value is ever an instance of this class
   = help: this `Core` class declares neither a property nor an instance member, so it is a name for calling members through rather than a class values are made of — a `Core` class that does have instances, `Core\Time\Date` among them, is testable like any declared one

error[E0303]: `Undeclared` is not declared
   --> %s:29:25
   |
29 | bool $e = $m instanceof Undeclared;
   |                         ^^^^^^^^^^ no matching declaration

error: aborting due to 5 errors
